# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: autocad-com-fails-in-disconnected-windows-session — COM/GUI CAD dies when the runner's desktop is suspended, headless batch survives
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: ci-infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: cad-session-state-probe
#+DATE: 2026-09-28

* Observed

Two benchmark jobs on the SAME Windows runner (PF5S26B), the SAME minute
(2026-09-28 17:35–17:36Z):

| job                          | id          | result  | how far                              |
|------------------------------+-------------+---------+--------------------------------------|
| benchmark:bricscad:windows   | 16783323457 | success | all 7 benchmarks, summary, exit 0    |
| benchmark:autocad:windows    | 16783323458 | FAILED  | 2 of 7 (arithmetic, lists) then died |

AutoCAD was NOT a COM-startup failure: the trace shows =implementation :
Visual LISP 2022 (fr)=, i.e. AutoCAD launched and AutoLISP executed. It ran
=arithmetic= and =lists=, then the process died mid-suite (~5 s hang) and
=make -C autolisp-benchmark run-autocad-windows= returned Error 1. BricsCAD
(=LispEx : Version 25.0=) ran the whole suite to completion on the same
machine.

* Cause (pjb, from the machine)

"The runner user account is logged-in, but the GUI session is suspended, back
to the login window / change user." AutoCAD's benchmark path drives the
application by COM automation, and *COM automation of a GUI application needs
a live interactive desktop*. When the session is disconnected/locked, the
desktop is gone and the GUI-dependent parts of AutoCAD fault partway through.
BricsCAD's path completed before touching anything that needs the desktop, or
tolerated it.

In alfe terms this is the =--mode automation= (COM bridge via =cscript=) vs
=--mode batch= (=accoreconsole=, headless, no COM) divide documented in the
alfe user manual: automation needs the desktop, batch does not. =--epure=
drives COM on AutoCAD, so it is in the automation camp.

* What this ticket adds

Not a fix for AutoCAD -- the runner's session state is an environment fact,
not a code defect -- but the instrumentation to SEE it and to characterise
what survives it:

1. =scripts/windows-session-state.ps1= — prints the interactive-session
   state: process vs console session id, WTS connect state (Active /
   Disconnected / …), window-station name (=console= vs =rdp-tcp#N=), and
   whether =LogonUI.exe= is up (locked / at the login screen). It ends with a
   one-line, greppable =SESSION-STATE: transport=… connect=… locked=… =>
   VERDICT= and always exits 0.

2. Every Windows CAD job now logs it. =.cad-sweep-before= (which every
   Windows CAD job extends) runs it FIRST, before the orphan sweep, so a CAD
   job's log says whether the desktop was live -- turning "Error 1" into
   "Error 1, and the desktop was DISCONNECTED".

3. =probe:cad-session-state:windows= (manual, CAD-tagged) — runs a 3x3
   matrix in the current session state: {clautolisp (in-process control),
   bricscad, autocad} x {=--mode batch=, =--mode automation=, =--epure=},
   recording each cell's exit + marker + whether it reached READY, and prints
   a table to read against the session banner. It reports and exits 0.

* Expected shape of the result (hypothesis, to confirm by running the probe)

- clautolisp: OK in every state (in-process, no desktop).
- =--mode batch=: OK even disconnected (accoreconsole is headless; BricsCAD
  batch already proven so by benchmark:bricscad:windows).
- =--mode automation= / =--epure=: FAIL or REACHED-READY-THEN-FAILED while
  disconnected/locked; OK on a live desktop.

If confirmed, the operational conclusion is that COM/GUI CAD jobs need the
runner's desktop kept CONNECTED (e.g. an RDP session left attached, or
autologon without lock), and that automated CAD verification that must run
unattended should prefer the batch/accoreconsole path where the vendor offers
one.

* RDP note

The detector distinguishes transport (=console= vs =rdp-tcp#N=) and connect
state, because RDP has its own version of this: an =rdp-tcp= session is
=Active= while the client is attached and =Disconnected= the moment the client
detaches WITHOUT logging off -- the same desktop-gone condition as a locked
console, and it breaks COM CAD the same way. A CAD job that works while
someone is RDP'd in and fails after they disconnect is this, not flakiness.

* Acceptance criteria

- =probe:cad-session-state:windows= run in the disconnected state produces a
  matrix distinguishing the engines/modes that survive it from those that do
  not, read against a =SESSION-STATE:= line that correctly names the state.
- Every Windows CAD job's log contains the =SESSION-STATE:= line.
- The detector correctly reports =locked= at the login screen and the
  =rdp-tcp= transport under RDP (confirmed on the machine).
- (added 2026-10-07, pjb) The disconnected matrix is measured in BOTH
  network contexts: with the VPN dropping with the session (PF5S26B, job
  16990501680 -- done) AND with NO VPN, license servers still reachable while
  the session is disconnected (the Windows Server runner), so that the
  desktop is the only variable. No license dialog may be waiting on
  reconnection for a no-VPN row to count.

* Note

Filed while comparing benchmark:autocad:windows with
benchmark:bricscad:windows at pjb's request. The scripts run only on the
Windows runner; they are validated there, not in the Linux lanes.

* Merged in: alfe-autocad-createobject-fails-on-runner (closed 2026-09-28)

The same morning, every =alfe --autocad= COM run in =verify:epure-api:windows=
(MR !336, job 16774481586) waited ~120 s and exited 4 with =ATTACHED=0
CREATED=0= -- =CreateObject= failed -- while BricsCAD ran on the same machine;
in the afternoon (MR !341, job 16776999021, alfe 2.2.146) AutoCAD started in
every run. A suspended desktop explains both. If it recurs, alfe >= 2.2.146
prints the COM error (it was being dropped: CP1252 error file read as UTF-8,
alfe-autocad-bridge-com-error-dropped-on-ansi); ~120 s then 0x80080005
(CO_E_SERVER_EXEC_FAILURE) would confirm it.

One thing to check here, noted when the two were compared:
=benchmark:autocad:windows= runs =--mode batch= (accoreconsole, headless, no
COM -- =autolisp-benchmark/Makefile=, =run-autocad-windows=), so its
mid-suite death may need another explanation than the missing desktop.

* Baseline run, desktop LIVE (2026-10-06, job 16959486114)

probe:cad-session-state:windows ran with the desktop unlocked:
"SESSION-STATE: transport=console connect=Active locked=no on-console=yes
=> DESKTOP-LIVE". All nine cells OK -- {clautolisp, bricscad, autocad} x
{batch, automation, epure} (clautolisp 5 / 0 / 0 s, bricscad 12 / 10 / 22 s,
autocad 9 / 34 / 23 s). This is the control row; the disconnected / locked
row is still to run (two attempts on 2026-10-05 were cancelled by master
pipelines, then the runner slept). Play the job again with the session
locked.

* Locked run, screen LOCKED with Win-L (2026-10-07, job 16984618487)

pjb locked PF5S26B (Win-L) with the runner still running in the GUI
session, then the job was played. The detector saw it: "SESSION-STATE:
transport=console connect=Active locked=yes on-console=yes => LOCKED"
(LogonUI present). All nine cells OK, marker printed, exit 0:

| engine     | batch | automation (COM) | epure |
|------------+-------+------------------+-------|
| clautolisp | 1 s   | 0 s              | 0 s   |
| bricscad   | 16 s  | 1 s              | 23 s  |
| autocad    | 14 s  | 31 s             | 25 s  |

So the hypothesis is REFUTED for the lock screen: a locked console session
keeps its window station and desktop (WTS state stays Active), and COM
automation of AutoCAD and BricsCAD works in it. The detector's LOCKED verdict
said "COM/GUI CAD expected to FAIL"; it now says what was measured, and it
tests Disconnected BEFORE locked (a switched-user session is usually both,
and Disconnected is the state that matters).

What remains unmeasured is the state pjb first described: "back to the login
window / CHANGE USER". Switching user (or detaching an RDP client) leaves the
runner's session Disconnected, which is a different thing from locked. To
measure it: from the runner's session, disconnect it (Win-L then "Switch
user", or run tsdiscon in a terminal of that session), then play
probe:cad-session-state:windows. If that row is also all OK, the 2026-09-28
benchmark:autocad:windows death needs another explanation -- note that that
job runs --mode batch (accoreconsole), which this ticket's own analysis
already flagged.

* Disconnected run, user SWITCHED (2026-10-07, job 16990501680)

pjb switched user on PF5S26B, leaving the runner's session disconnected, then
the job was replayed. Detector: "SESSION-STATE: transport=unknown
connect=Disconnected locked=yes on-console=no" -- the old verdict text named
it LOCKED, the ordering bug fixed above.

| engine     | batch                 | automation (COM)       | epure                 |
|------------+-----------------------+------------------------+-----------------------|
| clautolisp | OK 2 s                | OK 0 s                 | OK 0 s                |
| bricscad   | TIMEOUT 302 s, exit 75 | OK 2 s (see below)    | TIMEOUT 301 s, exit 75 |
| autocad    | OK 14 s               | TIMEOUT 191 s, exit 75 | TIMEOUT 190 s, exit 75 |

Every timeout is alfe's READY-TIMEOUT with last status "BOOTING": the engine
was launched (AutoCAD by the cscript COM bridge, BricsCAD as =bricscad.exe
/Automation /b run.scr=) and never evaluated alfe's bootstrap. (The probe's
table labelled these REACHED-READY-THEN-FAILED because it matched the bare
word READY, which alfe's timeout messages contain; it now matches "READY
after".)

The BricsCAD automation OK is not a fresh launch: it reached READY 0.79 s
after the bridge started, against 10-23 s for a cold BricsCAD start in every
other run. The bridge attached to the BricsCAD the timed-out batch run had
launched -- that process answered COM (and evaluated alfe's files) while
disconnected, though it never ran its /b script. A cold BricsCAD COM launch
in a disconnected session is therefore not established either way.

* Conclusion, for a runner whose VPN drops with the session (pjb, 2026-10-07)

When pjb reconnected after the run, two dialogs were waiting on the desktop:

- Bricsys license manager: "La licence n'est plus valide. L'application va
  maintenant s'arreter." (the license is no longer valid; the application
  will now stop);
- FlexNet License Finder: "Your application was not able to obtain a license
  because the FlexNet license server manager could not determine where to
  find the licensing data it needs" (specify the license server / file).

On PF5S26B the license servers (the FlexNet one EPURE uses, on the
enterprise LAN) are reached through a VPN, and disconnecting the user
disconnects the VPN too (it tries to reconnect as the new user). So on this
runner a disconnected session means no desktop AND no license server, and
pjb's ruling is to keep the conclusion in that context:

- locked console session (Win-L, VPN up): everything works, COM included
  (job 16984618487) -- locking is safe;
- disconnected session (switch user; a detached RDP client is the same WTS
  state), VPN dropping with it: AutoCAD COM (automation, epure) and BricsCAD's
  GUI launches (batch /b, epure) never get past BOOTING; only the headless
  paths work -- clautolisp and AutoCAD --mode batch (accoreconsole)
  (job 16990501680). The BricsCAD timeouts coincide with its "license no
  longer valid" dialog, a modal dialog on a desktop nobody sees.

Operational rule for PF5S26B: lock the screen; never switch user or leave an
RDP client detached while CAD jobs may run. Unattended CAD verification that
must survive a disconnect uses AutoCAD --mode batch. A Windows CAD job that
dies with READY-TIMEOUT "BOOTING" under connect=Disconnected is this, not a
code defect.

Which of the two causes -- the missing desktop or the missing license server
-- breaks the COM / GUI launches is NOT separated by this run.

The 2026-09-28 benchmark:autocad:windows death is not explained by this: it
ran --mode batch (accoreconsole), which survives a disconnected session.

* Next: the NO-VPN case, on the Windows Server runner (required, see the acceptance criteria)

Replay probe:cad-session-state:windows in a disconnected session on the
Windows Server machine, whose license servers are reachable without a VPN,
so that the desktop is the only variable. On reconnection, check that no
license dialog is waiting before trusting the rows.

Remaining for acceptance, as before: the rdp-tcp transport report under RDP.
